    <name>
      <text>Directional Derivative of a Function in a Given Direction</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Determine \(D_{u}f\) for \({@function@}\)  in the direction of  \(v({@point_x@},{@point_y@},{@point_z@})\)</p>
<p><b>Step \(1\):</b> We need to compute, \(\displaystyle\frac{d f(x,y,z)}{dx})=\triangledown f_x, \frac{d f(x,y,z)}{dy})=\triangledown f_y, \frac{d f(x,y,z)}{dz})=\triangledown f_z\). </p>
<br>
<p>\(\triangledown f_x=\)[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>\(\triangledown f_y=\)[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]][[feedback:prt2]]</p>
<p>\(\triangledown f_z=\)[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]][[feedback:prt3]]</p>
<br>
<p><b>Step \(2\):</b>  Now compute the direction vector</p>
<p>Recall that we need to make sure that the direction vector is a unit vector. </p>
<p>It is pretty clear that this vector is not a unit vector so let’s convert it to a unit vector.</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle ||\vec{v}||=\sqrt{{x^2}+{y^2}+{z^2}}=\)  [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]][[feedback:prt4]]</p>
<br>
<p>\(\displaystyle\vec{u}=\frac{\vec{u}}{||\vec{v}||}=\) [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]][[feedback:prt5]]</p>
<br>
<p><b>Step \(3\):</b>  Determine the directional derivative</p>
<p>\(\qquad \qquad=\) [[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]][[feedback:prt6]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked soultion</b></p>
<p>Determine \(D_{u}f\) for \({@function@}\)  in the direction of  \(v({@point_x@},{@point_y@},{@point_z@})\)</p>
<p><b>Step \(1\):</b> We need to compute, \(\displaystyle\frac{d f(x,y,z)}{dx})=\triangledown f_x, \frac{d f(x,y,z)}{dy})=\triangledown f_y, \frac{d f(x,y,z)}{dz})=\triangledown f_z\). </p>
<br>
<p>\(\triangledown f_x= {@ta_1@}\)</p>
<p>\(\triangledown f_y= {@ta_2@}\)</p>
<p>\(\triangledown f_z= {@ta_3@}\)</p>
<br>
<p><b>Step \(2\):</b>  Now compute the direction vector</p>
<p>Recall that we need to make sure that the direction vector is a unit vector. </p>
<p>It is pretty clear that this vector is not a unit vector so let’s convert it to a unit vector.</p>
<p>
\(\begin{align} 
\displaystyle ||\vec{v}|| &=\sqrt{{{@point_x@}^2}+{{@point_y@}^2}+{{@point_z@}^2}} \\
                                     &= {@ta_4@} \\
\displaystyle\vec{u} &=\frac{\vec{u}}{||\vec{v}||} \\
                               &= {@1/ta_4@}[{@point_x@},{@point_y@},{@point_z@}]  \\
                            &=  {@ta_5@}
\end{align}
\)
</p>
<br>
<p><b>Step \(3\):</b> The directional derivative is then,/p>
<p>
\(\begin{align} 
\displaystyle D_{u}f &={@gradient_F@} \cdot {@ta_5@} \\
                                     &= {@ta_6@} \\
\end{align}
\)
</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
